## Article 32

The competent police administration maintains records on reported cases of domestic violence and on the imposition and execution of urgent measures and execution of protection measures against domestic violence.
The police administration record contains:
1) data on reported cases of domestic violence (participants in the event, time, place, collected statements, circumstances of the case, data on the possible victim, etc.);
2) data on the reported possible perpetrator (name, surname, unique personal identification number, address of residence or stay, data on previously imposed protection measures against domestic violence);
3) data on risk assessment and names of bodies to which the risk assessment was submitted;
4) data on the imposition of urgent measures (date and number of the order imposing urgent measures, their duration and time of commencement of their duration);
5) data on the extension and execution of urgent measures (number and date of the basic court decision on extension of urgent measures, data on execution of urgent measures);
6) data on the execution of protection measures against domestic violence.
The basic court maintains a record of its decisions on proposals for extension of urgent measures and on imposed protection measures against domestic violence.
The basic court record on proposals for extension of urgent measures contains:
1) data on the person for whom the urgent measure was extended (name, surname, unique personal identification number, address of residence or stay, data on previously imposed protection measures against domestic violence);
2) number and date of the decision by which the urgent measure was extended;
3) number and date of the decision by which the proposal to extend the urgent measure was rejected;
4) data on the appeal against the decision made on the proposal for extension of urgent measures;
5) data on the decision made on the appeal.
The basic court record on imposed protection measures against domestic violence contains:
1) data on the person for whom a protection measure against domestic violence was imposed (name, surname, unique personal identification number, address of residence or stay, data on previously imposed protection measures against domestic violence);
2) data on the court decision on imposing protection measures against domestic violence (number and date of the decision, type of measure imposed and its duration);
3) data on the appeal against the court decision on imposing protection measures against domestic violence;
4) data on the decision made on the appeal;

5) data on the extension or termination of protection measures against domestic violence.
The basic public prosecutor's office maintains a record of proposals for extension of urgent measures and requests for imposing protection measures against domestic violence.
The basic public prosecutor's office record contains:
1) data on the person for whom extension of urgent measures was proposed (name, surname, unique personal identification number, address of residence or stay, data on previously imposed protection measures against domestic violence);
2) data on the extension of urgent measures (date and number of the proposal for extension of urgent measures, name of the court to which extension of urgent measures was proposed, court decision on the public prosecutor's proposal, data on the decision on appeal);
3) data on filing a lawsuit for imposing protection measures against domestic violence;
4) type of protection measure against domestic violence whose imposition is requested;
5) data on the court decision regarding the lawsuit for imposing a protection measure against domestic violence (number and date of the decision, type of measure imposed and duration of the measure);
6) data on the extension and termination of the protection measure against domestic violence.
The competent social welfare center maintains a record on the application of individual protection and support plans for the victim. The social welfare center record contains:
1) name, surname, unique personal identification number and address of residence or stay of the victim;
2) data on the individual protection and support plan for the victim;
3) data on planned victim protection measures;
4) data on planned victim support measures;
5) data on executors of specific protection and support measures and deadlines for their implementation;
6) data on the monitoring plan and assessment of effectiveness of planned and implemented measures.
The records of police administrations, basic courts, basic public prosecutor's offices and social welfare centers are maintained in electronic form and constitute the Central Record on Cases of Domestic Violence (hereinafter: Central Record), which is maintained by the Republic Public Prosecutor's Office.
Data may be entered into the Central Record only with the use of appropriate protected access codes. Data are kept in the records and in the Central Record for ten years and are deleted thereafter.
Access to records

## Article 33

Access to data from the Central Record is permitted only for the purpose of exercising the competences provided by this law and with the use of protected access codes.
The deputy public prosecutor who exercises the competences of the public prosecutor's office in preventing domestic violence and prosecuting perpetrators of criminal offenses specified by this law (

## Article 9) has the right to access all data from the Central Record

The competent police officer has the right to access the Central Record only in the part containing data from the records of regional police administrations and competent social welfare centers, competent courts only in the part containing data from records maintained by basic courts, and competent social welfare centers - only in the part containing data from records maintained by social welfare centers.

Protection of personal data

## Article 34

State bodies and institutions competent for the application of this law are obliged to protect personal data, in accordance with the law governing the protection of personal data.
For the collection of data contained in the records, the consent of the person to whom the data relates is not required.
VIII MONITORING THE APPLICATION OF THE LAW
Council for Combating Domestic Violence

## Article 35

The Government establishes the Council for Combating Domestic Violence (hereinafter: Council), which monitors the application of this law and improves coordination and effectiveness of preventing domestic violence and protection against domestic violence.
The members of the Council consist of representatives of state bodies and institutions competent for the application of this law.
The Council may, as needed, include in its work representatives of scientific and other professional institutions and associations whose activities are related to protection against domestic violence.
The composition, manner of work and decision-making of the Council shall be regulated in more detail by the Government act on the establishment of the Council.
IX PENAL PROVISIONS
Misdemeanors

## Article 36

A person who violates an urgent measure imposed or extended on them shall be punished for a misdemeanor by imprisonment of up to 60 days.
A responsible person in a state or other body, organization or institution who does not immediately report to the police or public prosecutor or does not respond to a report or obstructs the reporting or response to any knowledge of domestic violence or imminent danger thereof (

## Article 13 paragraph 2) shall be punished for a misdemeanor by a fine from 50,000 dinars to 150,000 dinars

A conviction judgment for the misdemeanor from paragraph 1 of this Article may be executed before it becomes final, in accordance with the Law on Misdemeanors.
X TRANSITIONAL AND FINAL PROVISIONS
Deadline for adoption of bylaws

## Article 37

Bylaws provided by this law shall be adopted within three months from the entry into force of this law, except for acts adopted by coordination and cooperation groups which shall be adopted within 30 days from the date of their establishment.

Deadline for establishment of coordination and cooperation groups and the Council and appointment of designated liaison persons

## Article 38

By the date of commencement of application of this law, coordination and cooperation groups and the Council shall be established and designated liaison persons shall be appointed.

Final provision

## Article 39

This law enters into force on the eighth day from the date of publication in the "Official Gazette of the Republic of Serbia", and shall be applied from
1 June 2017.
